/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A combinational circuit is easy to test: apply a vector at the pins, wait, compare the outputs.
   - Design for testability (DFT) means adding hardware or following rules so the chip is cheap to test.
   - These help, but they give no systematic method.
   - The standard muxed-D scan cell puts a 2:1 multiplexer in front of a D flip-flop.
   - Full scan makes every flip-flop a scan cell.
   - The costs: a multiplexer and extra wiring per flip-flop (a few per cent of logic area), one multiplexer delay on every functional D path, an SE net that reaches every cell, and two or more extra pins.
   - Because shift-out of one pattern overlaps shift-in of the next, P patterns on a chain of N cells take
   - where N is the length of the longest chain, P the number of patterns, the 1 is the capture clock, and the last N is the final shift-out.
   - Splitting the flip-flops into K balanced chains cuts N to about N ÷ K, so test time falls almost K times, but each chain needs its own scan-in and scan-out pin.
   - Answer.
*/
