/* book Worked example 2: Q: A design has 200 flip-flops in one scan chain and needs 1000 scan patterns. The scan clock is 100 MHz. Find the total test time. Then split the flip-flops into 4 balanced chains of 50 and find it again.
   1. Clock period = 1 ÷ 100 MHz = 10 ns.
   2. One chain, N = 200: cycles = (200 + 1) × 1000 + 200 = 201,000 + 200 = 201,200. Time = 201,200 × 10 ns = 2.012 ms.
   3. Per pattern: 201 cycles = 2.01 µs, of which 2.00 µs is shifting.
   4. Four chains, N = 50: cycles = (50 + 1) × 1000 + 50 = 51,050. Time = 51,050 × 10 ns = 0.5105 ms.
   5. Speed-up = 2.012 ÷ 0.5105 = 3.94.
   Answer: 2.012 ms with one chain and 0.511 ms with four chains, 3.94 times faster, at the price of three more scan-in and three more scan-out pins. */
