/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - JTAG adds a test access port (TAP) to the chip.
   - A boundary-scan cell sits between the core logic and each digital signal pad (power, ground and analog pins have none).
   - Why at the pin?
   - The TAP controller is a 16-state machine that moves on each rising edge of TCK according to TMS.
   - Every compliant chip has the instruction register (IR, at least 2 bits), the 1-bit bypass register and the BSR; most also have a 32-bit IDCODE register that returns the maker and part number.
   - On a board the chips form a daisy chain: the board's TDI goes to the first chip's TDI, each TDO feeds the next TDI, and the last TDO is the board's TDO.
   - where L is the number of bits shifted in one pass of Shift-IR or Shift-DR and Lᵢ is the length of the register selected in chip i.
   - In an interconnect test the tester loads EXTEST into every chip, preloads drive values, then for each pattern captures the received bits and shifts them out while the next pattern shifts in.
*/
