/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - BIST wraps three blocks around the circuit under test (CUT).
   - Vectors reach the CUT in two ways.
   - where T is the BIST time in clocks, N the number of vectors, L the length of the longest scan chain, and the 1 the capture clock.
   - A linear feedback shift register (LFSR) is a shift register whose input is the XOR of a few chosen stages, the taps.
   - where n is the number of stages.
   - The all-zero word is a lock-up state: XOR of zeros is zero, so the register would stay there for ever.
   - Some faults need a vector a plain LFSR rarely makes; they are called random-pattern-resistant.
   - A multiple-input signature register (MISR) is an LFSR with one extra XOR in front of each stage, where one CUT output enters each clock.
   - where P(aliasing) is the chance that a faulty CUT still ends with the golden signature and n is the MISR width.
   - BIST comes in flavours by its TPG: exhaustive (a counter applies all 2ᵐ vectors to an m-input block), pseudo-exhaustive (each output cone gets all combinations of its own inputs), pseudo-random (the LFSR stream) and deterministic (stored or
*/
